.TH FCLOSE 3
.SH NAME
fclose, fflush \- close or flush a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

int
fclose(stream)
FILE *stream;

int
fflush(stream)
FILE *stream;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fflush
writes out what is waiting in the buffer of a stream that is open for
writing, and leaves the stream open. It does nothing to a stream that is
being read.
.PP
.B fclose
flushes the stream, closes its file, and frees the buffer stdio gave it.
The FILE structure can then be used again by
.BR fopen (3).
.PP
.BR exit (2),
and a return from
.BR main ,
flush every stream that is open for writing, so a program need not
close its files before it ends.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
Both return 0 on success, and EOF if a write failed.
.B fclose
also returns EOF if the stream was not open, or the
.BR close (2)
failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
close(2), exit(2), fopen(3), setbuf(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B fclose
function first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX. A predecessor,
.BR flush ,
first appeared in Version 1, and the
.B fflush
function in Version 4.
